In `MauiProgram.cs`, use conditional compilation or the `AddPlatformServices` pattern:

```csharp
public static MauiApp CreateMauiApp()
{
    var builder = MauiApp.CreateBuilder();
    builder.UseMauiApp<App>();

#if ANDROID
    builder.Services.AddSingleton<INotificationService, Platforms.Android.AndroidNotificationService>();
#elif IOS
    builder.Services.AddSingleton<INotificationService, Platforms.iOS.iOSNotificationService>();
#elif WINDOWS
    builder.Services.AddSingleton<INotificationService, Platforms.Windows.WindowsNotificationService>();
#endif

    return builder.Build();
}
```

Each implementation lives under `Platforms/<OS>/` and is only compiled for its target (the default `<TargetFramework>` filtering handles that).

**Alternative** — keep `MauiProgram.cs` clean by using partial classes:

```csharp
// MauiProgram.cs
builder.Services.AddPlatformServices();

// Services/PlatformServiceExtensions.cs (shared partial)
public static partial class PlatformServiceExtensions
{
    public static partial IServiceCollection AddPlatformServices(this IServiceCollection services);
}

// Platforms/Android/PlatformServiceExtensions.cs
public static partial class PlatformServiceExtensions
{
    public static partial IServiceCollection AddPlatformServices(this IServiceCollection services)
    {
        services.AddSingleton<INotificationService, AndroidNotificationService>();
        return services;
    }
}
```

Repeat the partial for iOS/Windows. This won't break on any platform because only the matching partial is compiled per target framework.
